==Latin==

===Etymology===
Per the LIV and IEW, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*snewbʰ-||to marry, to wed}}, cognate to {{cog|sla-pro|*snubìti}}<ref>{{R:ine:IEW|head=sneubh|pages=977|volume=III}}</ref><ref>{{R:ine:LIV|head=*snewbʰ-|page=574}}</ref>. Ernout and Meillet dispute this and instead connect this word with {{m|la|nūbēs||cloud}}, from PIE {{m|ine-pro|*(s)newdʰ-||to cover}}.<ref name="EM">{{R:la:EM|page=449}}</ref> De Vaan finds Ernout and Meillet's proposal semantically attractive but morphologically difficult: if the stem originally ended in ''*dʰ'', then the attested supine stem must be a recent (re)formation, since an old supine form would have regularly developed ''-ss-'', as in {{m|la|iussus|pos=perfect participle of {{m|la|iubeō}}}} from {{m|ine-pro|*Hyewdʰ-}}.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=nūbō|page=417}}</ref> 

The vowel in the first syllable of the supine stem is marked long by Lewis (1891) and Bennett (1907)<ref>{{cite-book|author=Charles E. Bennett|title=The Latin Language – a historical outline of its sounds, inflections, and syntax|chapter=Hidden Quantity|location=Boston|publisher=Allyn and Bacon|year=1907|page=70|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/latinlanguagehis00bennuoft/page/61|authorlink=Charles E. Bennett (scholar)}}</ref>, but De Vaan (2008) implies that it is short by omitting a macron, Ernout and Meillet explicitly mark it with a breve (''nŭptum''),<ref name="EM"></ref> and Wartburg (1928–2002) and Bienvenu (1965) mark ŭ as short in the derived word {{m|la|nuptiae}}.<ref>{{R:FEW|7|245|nŭptiae}}</ref><ref>Bienvenu, Emmett, "The Stem-Vowel Quantity of the Nouns, Adjectives and Verbs Used by Virgil and Horace" (1965). Master's Theses. 1909. Page 71. https://ecommons.luc.edu/luc_theses/1909</ref> A short vowel in the supine stem would match the old ablaut-based pattern seen in {{m|la|dūcō}}, {{m|la|dūxī}}, {{m|la|ductum}}; on the other hand, a long vowel could have been introduced by analogy with the present stem, perfect stem, or both (as in {{m|la|scrībō}}, {{m|la|scrīpsī}}, {{m|la|scrīptum}}).

Possibly cognate with {{cog|grc|νύμφη||bride, young wife, nymph}} (English {{m|en|nymph}}), but this is disputed.

